title: Fix Ok/Err ctor monomorphization collision for Result of same T, different
  E
status: complete
atomic: true
priority: high
blocked_by: []
created_at: '2026-06-06T00:23:44'
updated_at: '2026-06-07T20:04:59'
result: Fixed Ok/Err ctor monomorphization collision via is_result_variant_expr tail-wrap
  passing full Result enum as expected type; each function binds its own Result mono.
  Verified end-to-end (8,2,3 cross-layer From test).
description: 'Pre-existing bug (discovered during 2-4-4): when two `Result of T, E`
  instantiations share the same success type T but differ in E (e.g. `Result of i64,
  NetError` and `Result of i64, FileError`), the `Ok(x)`/`Err(x)` variant constructor
  inside a function resolves to the WRONG monomorphized Result enum. Repro: define
  `fetch returns Result of i64, NetError` with `Ok(7)` and `save returns Result of
  i64, FileError` with `Ok(1)`; `fetch`''s MIR wraps `Ok(7)` as `Result__G_i64_FileError`
  (wrong), causing `Function return type does not match operand` LLVM verify failure.
  Root cause likely in variant_tags / Ok-wrap resolution keyed only by success type
  T (or variant name) ignoring E. The From-conversion propagation path (2-4-4) is
  correct; this is independent. Affects auto-wrap-Ok and tail-wrap. Done: each function''s
  Ok/Err ctor binds the Result monomorphization matching its declared return type.'
